{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "297959425",
    "title": "Skills — What They Are and How to Use Them",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/297959425",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 7,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "This is the canonical reference for every skill in this folder. If you're looking for *why*\nthe skill system exists (the human-in-the-lead design philosophy), see Human-in-the-Lead AI Development — Project Setup Overview —\nthat page covers the rationale; this file covers the *mechanics*: what each skill does, what\nto pass it, and what it hands back.\n\nHow skills work\n---------------\n\n* Invoke a skill by typing `/skill-name` (optionally followed by an argument) in a Claude\n  Code session. Each skill is a structured, reviewable process defined in its own\n  `<skill-name>/SKILL.md` file — not a freeform prompt.\n* Where a skill takes an argument, its `SKILL.md` uses `$ARGUMENTS` to capture whatever\n  follows the skill name, e.g. `/new-component productBadge` or `/audit-tier overrides/app/hooks/use-wish-list.ts`.\n  If you invoke a skill with no argument and one is expected, Claude asks for it.\n* Every skill is **human-in-the-lead**: it either stops for confirmation at a defined\n  checkpoint, or produces a read-only report/plan rather than making changes outright.\n  See the checkpoint column in each table below.\n* These `SKILL.md` files are also the master source for `.cursor/rules/skills/*.mdc` and\n  `.vscode/instructions/workflows.md` — see [scripts/syncCursorRules.mjs](../../scripts/syncCursorRules.mjs).\n  Edit the `SKILL.md` here, then run `npm run sync:ai-rules:fix`; never edit the generated\n  Cursor/VS Code files directly.\n\nSetup & onboarding\n------------------\n\n| Skill | Usage | What it does | Example on this project | Human-in-the-lead checkpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [/onboarding](onboarding/SKILL.md) | `/onboarding` | Interactive walkthrough for a new developer or QA engineer — role and squad questions, mechanical setup checks (git hooks, Node version, branch), then role-specific orientation to the rules and tools. Run on a **first session on a new machine or first day on the project**. | A new Squad B (Product) developer's first session: confirms Node version, checks out `develop`, then walks through the Product-owned files in `protectedFiles.md` (`product-tile`, `display-price`, `swatch-group`). | Confirms understanding at each phase before moving on |\n| [/session-start](session-start/SKILL.md) | `/session-start` | Confirms the current branch is safe to work on, reads `.docs/memory/` tracking docs, runs health checks, and states current project position. Run at the **start of every session**. | Run first thing on `fix/checkout-address-validation` — confirms you're not on `develop`/`main`, then surfaces open items like the Global-e checkout ownership decision from `projectState.md`. | Waits for you to confirm direction before writing any code |\n| [/project-init](project-init/SKILL.md) | `/project-init` | One-time project setup walkthrough — runs `scripts/projectInitCheck.sh`, fixes automatable failures, then asks one question at a time for items that need a human answer (Jira prefix, BrowserStack, secrets, Zephyr product, etc.), updating the [7.1c CI/CD & Environment Setup Checklist](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/305430530) Confluence page (via the Atlassian MCP tools) as it goes. | Setting up a fresh clone: confirms the `ZIM` Jira prefix, BrowserStack creds for cross-browser Playwright runs, and the Zephyr test-management project key. | Asks one question at a time, waits for the answer before the next |\n\nPlanning & safety\n-----------------\n\n| Skill | Usage | What it does | Example on this project | Human-in-the-lead checkpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [/feature-spec](feature-spec/SKILL.md) | `/feature-spec <plain-English feature description>` | Turns a feature request into a scoped implementation spec **before any code is written** — clarifies requirements one question at a time, researches the codebase in parallel, checks sprint scope, then outputs a spec with file list, tier flags, and a ready-to-paste Claude Code prompt. | `/feature-spec Add a size-guide modal launched from the PDP image gallery` — checks `.docs/prd/` for an existing size-guide requirement and `sprintScope.md` before proposing files under `overrides/app/components/`. | Produces a plan only — you review the spec before implementation begins |\n| [/scope-check](scope-check/SKILL.md) | `/scope-check <a proposed Claude Code prompt>` | Reviews a prompt *before you run it* — classifies every file it would likely touch by protection tier, flags drift risk (refactoring upward, cross-squad reach), and verdicts it SAFE / RISKY / BLOCKED. Rewrites risky prompts into safely-scoped ones. | `/scope-check \"add a wishlist heart icon to the product tile\"` — flags `product-tile/` as Tier 2 (Squad B, notify Squad A) and verdicts RISKY until scoped to a single component. | You see the blast radius and verdict before the risky prompt is ever run |\n| [/audit-tier](audit-tier/SKILL.md) | `/audit-tier <file or directory path>` | Identifies the protection tier (1–4), owner, and import blast radius of a path under `overrides/`. Use before editing anything you're unsure about. | `/audit-tier overrides/app/hooks/use-current-basket.ts` — returns Tier 1, owner Integration/SI Partner, and lists every component that consumes basket state before you touch checkout logic. | Tells you who to notify before you touch the file |\n\nDevelopment\n-----------\n\n| Skill | Usage | What it does | Example on this project | Human-in-the-lead checkpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [/new-component](new-component/SKILL.md) | `/new-component <componentName>` | Scaffolds a new Zimmermann component under `overrides/app/components/` — TypeScript, Chakra-first, folder layout and props typing per `.claude/rules/conventions.md`, paired Storybook story, optional test file. | `/new-component sizeGuideModal` — scaffolds `overrides/app/components/sizeGuideModal/` with `index.tsx`, `index.stories.tsx` under `Forms/`, and a `SizeGuideModalProps` type. | You name the component and confirm the resulting file structure |\n\nQuality & review\n----------------\n\n| Skill | Usage | What it does | Example on this project | Human-in-the-lead checkpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [/code-quality](code-quality/SKILL.md) | `/code-quality [file or directory]` (no argument = files changed on the current branch) | **Read-only** audit against `.claude/rules/conventions.md` — naming/structure, circular imports, TypeScript coverage, test/story existence. Produces a prioritised 🔴/🟡/🔵 report. Use after implementing a feature or before opening a PR. | `/code-quality overrides/app/components/promo-code` — flags a missing `.stories.tsx` and a hardcoded hex colour instead of a theme token. | Report only — you decide what to fix and when |\n| [/pr-checklist](pr-checklist/SKILL.md) | `/pr-checklist` | Runs `/code-quality` on the current branch's changed files, classifies each by tier and reviewer requirement, and generates a copy-paste-ready PR description with a test checklist. Pairs with `/code-quality`. | On `fix/order-summary-tax-display`: generates a PR description noting `order-summary/` is Tier 2 (Squad A owner) so a non-author Squad A review is required before merge. | You review and submit the generated PR description — nothing is posted for you |\n\nQA / testing\n------------\n\n| Skill | Usage | What it does | Example on this project | Human-in-the-lead checkpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [/qa-generate](qa-generate/SKILL.md) | `/qa-generate` (then answers Jira story questions) | Two-step, human-gated E2E test generation: drafts a Gherkin `.feature` file and **stops completely**; only generates the Playwright spec after Lead QA has explicitly validated (and optionally annotated) the feature file. Use for any new storefront user journey needing E2E coverage. | `/qa-generate` against `ZIM-142` (Guest checkout on the AU site) — drafts `e2e/features/ZIM-142-guest-checkout.feature` across the AU/US/UK/EU/HK/ME site fixtures for Lead QA sign-off before any Playwright code exists. | Playwright spec is not generated until Lead QA signs off on the `.feature` file |\n\nContentful\n----------\n\n| Skill | Usage | What it does | Example on this project | Human-in-the-lead checkpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [/contentful-register-component](contentful-register-component/SKILL.md) | `/contentful-register-component <component file path>` | Reads an existing component's TypeScript props, generates a `*ContentfulDefinition.ts` file mapping each prop to a Contentful variable type, and wires it into the `defineComponents` registry. | `/contentful-register-component overrides/app/components/heroBanner/index.tsx` — maps `headline`, `ctaLabel`, and `backgroundImage` props to Contentful Studio variable types so merchandising can build homepage banners without a code change. | You review the generated definition (and any skipped/ambiguous props) before it's published in Contentful |\n| [/contentful-drift-check](contentful-drift-check/SKILL.md) | `/contentful-drift-check [component or directory path]` (no argument = scan all registered components) | **Read-only** comparison of component props against their paired Contentful definitions — reports added props, removed props, and type mismatches. Use after props change, or to audit everything at once. | `/contentful-drift-check overrides/app/components/productTile` — catches a newly added `showQuickAdd` prop that isn't yet exposed in the Contentful definition, before merchandising hits a silently-ignored field. | Report only — you resolve any drift found |\n\nRelease management\n------------------\n\n| Skill | Usage | What it does | Example on this project | Human-in-the-lead checkpoint |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| [/generate-changelog](generate-changelog/SKILL.md) | `/generate-changelog` (then answers version/range questions) | Builds a structured, four-audience changelog (dev team, QA, product, GitHub Release) from conventional commits and the upstream baseline. Writes to `docs/releases/`. Use before cutting a release branch. | Run before cutting `release/2.4.0` — produces a QA-facing summary calling out the new gift-card redemption flow so SIT can prioritise those test cases. | You review the changelog before it's published; the GitHub Release command is proposed, never run automatically |\n| [/upstream-sync-review](upstream-sync-review/SKILL.md) | `/upstream-sync-review` (then supply a candidate `@salesforce/retail-react-app` version) | Reviews upstream release notes against `.docs/memory/upstreamBaseline.md`, maps changes to our override surface, and recommends BUMP / DEFER / WAIT — per the \"read the changelog, bump opportunistically\" policy in `.docs/upstreamPolicy.md`. | `/upstream-sync-review 10.2.0` — checks whether the upstream `product-tile` changes conflict with our Tier 2 override before recommending BUMP / DEFER / WAIT. | Recommendation only — you approve and run the actual `npm install` bump |\n\nAdding a new skill\n------------------\n\n1. Create `.claude/skills/<skill-name>/SKILL.md` with `name` and `description` frontmatter (see any existing skill for the format).\n2. Add it to the appropriate table above.\n3. Add it to the skill list in [scripts/syncCursorRules.mjs](../../scripts/syncCursorRules.mjs) so it gets mirrored to `.cursor/rules/skills/` and `.vscode/instructions/workflows.md`, then run `npm run sync:ai-rules:fix`.\n4. If it belongs in the [Project Setup Overview](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/305102849) Confluence page's \"Part 2\" narrative, add a one-line mention there — but keep the detailed reference here to avoid duplication.\n\n---\n\n*Source:* `.claude/skills/README.md` *in the zimmermann-pwa repository, with SFCC-project-specific examples added for the Confluence reference page.*",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "emoji": "c92fb136-f084-45a3-9b2a-503c8101ba82",
    "page_width": "fixed-width"
  }
}"}

{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "304775192",
    "title": "Claude Code CLI — Developer Onboarding Guide",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/304775192",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 4,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "This guide is for any developer joining the Zimmermann SFCC build who will use Claude Code CLI. Read it before your first session — it will save you from the most common mistakes.\n\n> Architecture note: this is a slim overrides project on `@salesforce/retail-react-app` v10 — not a fork of the PWA Kit monorepo. All Zimmermann customisations live in `overrides/` and must be TypeScript (`.ts`/`.tsx`). The base template lives in `node_modules/@salesforce/retail-react-app/` and must not be edited.\n\n1. What Claude Code is — and isn't\n----------------------------------\n\n**What it is:** an AI coding agent that runs in your terminal. You give it a task in plain English, it reads your codebase, and it makes code changes directly to your files. It writes components, fixes bugs, adds tests, refactors logic, and explains code.\n\n**What it is not:** - It doesn't understand your team's current sprint, active branches, or verbal agreements - It doesn't know another developer is mid-refactor on the same hook - It doesn't know a shared utility is used by 14 other components - It has no opinion on whether a change is architecturally appropriate for Zimmermann - It will not warn you before overwriting something important unless you've told it what's important\n\n**Practical consequence:** if your prompt is under-scoped, Claude Code will make changes beyond what you intended — including to shared code other developers depend on. The rules in the repo's `AGENTS.md` exist to prevent this.\n\n2. Installation and authentication\n----------------------------------\n\nPrerequisites: Node.js 18/20/22/24 LTS, npm 9+, access to the Zimmermann GitHub org.\n\nDefaultbashnpm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code\nclaude login # opens a browser — use your work email\nclaude --version # verify\n\nRun your first session read-only: navigate to the repo root, run `claude`, and type \"Describe the structure of this codebase without modifying any files.\"\n\nTwo Git hooks (`pre-commit`, `commit-msg`) install automatically via Husky on `npm install`. Never bypass them with `git commit --no-verify`.\n\n3. Always do this before running Claude Code\n--------------------------------------------\n\n**3.0 —** New joiners run `/onboarding` first. `/project-init` is a project-level (not per-developer) check of what's still open — most developers won't need it.\n\n**3.1 — Run `/session-start`** at the start of every session. It reads the project's tracking docs, runs a health check script, states where the build is, and asks you to confirm direction before writing any code. Do not write code until it has completed and you've confirmed the next task.\n\n**3.2 — Confirm your branch:**`git branch --show-current`. If `develop` or `main`, stop and create a feature branch first.\n\n**3.3 — Pull the latest from develop:**`git pull origin develop`, even if you pulled this morning.\n\n**3.4 — Write your prompt before you open Claude Code.** It must include the target (feature/page/component, ideally exact file paths), what you want changed and why, and an explicit scope boundary (\"do not modify any file other than X\"). If you can't name the target or scope boundary, read more of the codebase first, or ask Claude a read-only \"explain this flow\" question before scoping the change.\n\n4. How to read AGENTS.md\n------------------------\n\nClaude Code auto-loads a small set of files at the start of every session:\n\n| File | Role |\n| --- | --- |\n| `CLAUDE.md` | Stub — re-exports `AGENTS.md` so Claude picks it up |\n| `AGENTS.md` | Branch rules, hard rules, stop-and-ask matrix, project-wide behaviours |\n| `.claude/rules/conventions.md` | Day-to-day code-writing conventions (naming, imports, components, hooks, stories) |\n| `.claude/rules/frozenFiles.md` | Tier 1 — frozen files |\n| `.claude/rules/protectedFiles.md` | Tier 2 — protected files |\n\nEverything else (PRDs, memory files, squad-specific scope) is loaded on demand by skills like `/session-start`, `/audit-tier`, or `/scope-check`.\n\n`AGENTS.md` at the repo root is the rulebook for any AI assistant on this project. The same rules are mirrored to Cursor and GitHub Copilot via a sync script.\n\n* **TypeScript only inside `overrides/`** — `.ts`/`.tsx` only; run `npx tsc --noEmit` before finishing any task that touches it.\n* **Hard Rules** — absolute prohibitions; if a task requires breaking one, it needs a human, not Claude Code.\n* **Tier 1 files** — frozen. Claude won't touch them without explicit instruction, and even then owner approval must be confirmed first.\n* **Tier 2 files** — require a second-developer PR review before merging to develop.\n* **Squad ownership** — Squad A owns checkout/cart/payments; Squad B owns PDP/PLP/search/account. Message the other squad's lead before touching their territory, even in your own squad's area if it overlaps.\n\n5. The branching rule — why it's non-negotiable\n-----------------------------------------------\n\nOn `develop`, a bad Claude Code session could break the integration environment for both squads, introduce regressions blocking the next deployment window, corrupt shared hook state, or trigger a failed Lighthouse CI gate. `develop` only receives human-reviewed, PR-approved changes.\n\n**There are no exceptions, including under deadline pressure.**\n\n`--dangerously-skip-permissions` bypasses all edit/shell-command confirmation prompts — it exists for CI automation only. **Never use it on `release/**`, `develop`, or `main`.** If you want to use it to speed up a session, that's a signal to make your prompt more specific, not to remove the safety check.\n\n**Fixing a bug on a release branch:** don't edit the release branch directly — branch off it:\n\nDefaultbashgit checkout release/v0.1.0\ngit pull origin release/v0.1.0\ngit checkout -b fix/release-v0.1.0\\_short-description\n# fix the bug, then:\ngh pr create --base release/v0.1.0 --title \"fix(scope): SFCC-999 short description\"\n\n6. How to scope a prompt — worked examples\n------------------------------------------\n\nThe quality of your prompt is the single biggest factor in whether Claude Code helps or causes problems.\n\n**Two flavours of \"specific\":** when you know exact file paths, name them — this is strictly safer and faster. When you don't, name the feature/entry point and the scope boundary, and let Claude expand to the full file list (it pauses for your confirmation).\n\n**Override-first workflow reminder:** if the file doesn't yet exist under `overrides/`, don't bundle the copy + functional change into one prompt — copy and convert first, confirm the override renders, then make the functional change.\n\n**Example — building a new component:**\n\nDefaultnoneCreate a new file at overrides/app/components/backInStockButton/index.tsx\nBuild a React component that:\n- Accepts productId (string) and variantId (string) as props\n- Renders a Chakra UI Button with the label \"Notify Me\"\n- On click, calls a placeholder async function handleNotifyMe() that logs the productId and variantId\n- Uses Chakra's Button directly (no Tier 1/Tier 2 file imports)\n- Defines BackInStockButtonProps at the top of the file\n- Co-locate a paired Storybook story with title: 'Commerce/BackInStockButton' and a Default story\nDo not modify any existing files. I will wire this into the PDP myself.\n\n**Example — fixing a layout bug in an existing override:**\n\nDefaultnoneIn this file only: overrides/app/pages/checkout/partials/shipping-address.tsx\nThe Stack component wrapping the address form fields has too much vertical spacing on mobile.\nChange the spacing prop from 6 to 3 when the breakpoint is base (mobile).\nDo not modify any theme files. Do not modify any other checkout partials. Do not modify any hooks.\n\n**Example — read-only exploration:**\n\nDefaultnoneWithout modifying any files, explain how the Gift With Purchase (bonus product) flow works in\nthis codebase. Which hook manages the GWP state? Which component renders the selection modal?\nWhere is the eligibility rule evaluation happening? What SCAPI endpoint is being called?\nSearch both overrides/ and node\\_modules/@salesforce/retail-react-app/.\n\n7. If Claude Code modifies something it shouldn't\n-------------------------------------------------\n\n1. **Stop the session** — `Ctrl+C`.\n2. **Check what changed** — `git diff --name-only`.\n3. **Assess impact** — squad-scoped files: review carefully and continue. Tier 2: flag in the PR description. Tier 1: see step 4.\n4. **Revert Tier 1 changes immediately** — `git checkout HEAD -- path/to/the/file.ts`, or `git checkout .` to revert everything.\n5. **Notify the file owner** — even after reverting, they need to know it happened.\n6. **Refine your prompt and try again** — the most common cause is an under-scoped prompt; rewrite with explicit file paths and \"do not modify any other files.\"\n\n8. Who to contact\n-----------------\n\nSee [1.2 Team Overview](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/261586947) for the current team directory and contacts. Tier 1 and Tier 2 owners are listed in the repo's `AGENTS.md` Contact Map and `.claude/rules/` files.\n\n9. Quick reference card\n-----------------------\n\n| Scenario | What to do |\n| --- | --- |\n| Starting any Claude Code session | Run `/session-start` → check branch → pull develop → write scoped prompt |\n| You're on develop or main | Create a feature branch first, no exceptions |\n| You need to change a design token | Ask the UI designer — Claude Code does not touch Tier 1 theme files |\n| You need to change `useCurrentBasket` | Ask the Integration lead — Tier 1 |\n| You need to change a component the other squad owns | Message that squad's lead first |\n| Claude modified files you didn't intend | `git diff --name-only` → revert Tier 1 files → notify owners |\n| You're unsure if a file is shared | `grep -r \"import.*filename\" overrides/` |\n| You want Claude to explain code without changing it | Always say \"without modifying any files\" in your prompt |\n| You're hitting a Tier 1 file legitimately | Get owner approval → name the file explicitly → PR it |\n| You need a new package dependency | List what you need to the integration team — do not ask Claude to add it |\n| Your commit was rejected by the commit-msg hook | Fix the format: `type(scope): description` |\n| You introduced a `.js` file in `overrides/` | Stop. Convert to `.ts`/`.tsx` |\n\nAppendix — useful git commands\n------------------------------\n\nDefaultbashgit branch --show-current\ngit diff --name-only\ngit diff\ngit checkout HEAD -- path/to/file.ts\ngit checkout develop && git pull && git checkout -b feature/TICKET-description\ngit add path/to/file1.ts path/to/file2.ts",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "emoji": "c92fb136-f084-45a3-9b2a-503c8101ba82",
    "page_width": "fixed-width"
  }
}"}

