    },
    "version": 2,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "New developer machine setup — from a fresh clone to a running local storefront. Complete once before your first coding session.\n\nPrerequisites\n-------------\n\n| Tool | Required version | Check |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Node.js | 18, 20, 22, or 24 LTS | `node --version` |\n| npm | 9+ | `npm --version` |\n| Git | any recent | `git --version` |\n\nIf you need to manage multiple Node versions, [nvm](https://github.com/nvm-sh/nvm) (macOS/Linux) or [nvm-windows](https://github.com/coreybutler/nvm-windows) work well.\n\n1. Clone the repository\n-----------------------\n\nDefaultbashgit clone git@github.com:ZimmermannAU/zimmermann-pwa.git\ncd zimmermann-pwa\n\n2. Install dependencies\n-----------------------\n\nDefaultbashnpm ci\n\nThis also runs the Husky `prepare` script, which installs two Git hooks automatically:\n\n* **`pre-commit`** — checks for Contentful definition drift; blocks edits to derived AI-config files when their source is not also staged\n* **`commit-msg`** — enforces conventional commit format (`type(scope): description`)\n\nVerify the hooks are in place: `ls .husky/` should show `pre-commit commit-msg`. If missing (e.g. cloned with `--no-install`):\n\nDefaultbashcp scripts/hooks/pre-commit .git/hooks/pre-commit && chmod +x .git/hooks/pre-commit\ncp scripts/hooks/commit-msg .git/hooks/commit-msg && chmod +x .git/hooks/commit-msg\n\nDo not bypass the hooks with `git commit --no-verify`.\n\n3. Set up your environment file\n-------------------------------\n\nDefaultbashcp .env.sample .env\n\nThe most important variables to fill in before running locally:\n\n| Variable | Where to get it |\n| --- | --- |\n| `COMMERCE_API_CLIENT_ID` | Ask the Integration Lead |\n| `COMMERCE_API_ORGANIZATION_ID` | Ask the Integration Lead |\n| `COMMERCE_API_SHORT_CODE` | Ask the Integration Lead |\n| `COMMERCE_API_SITE_ID` | Ask the Integration Lead |\n| `CONTENTFUL_SPACE_ID` | In `.env.sample` (non-secret — already populated) |\n| `CONTENTFUL_DELIVERY_TOKEN` | Ask the Integration Lead |\n| `CONTENTFUL_PREVIEW_TOKEN` | Ask the Integration Lead (only needed for CMS preview) |\n\nMost other variables (Adyen, Narvar, Google reCAPTCHA, etc.) are not required to start the local dev server. `.env` is gitignored — never commit it, and never add token values to `.env.sample`.\n\n4. Start the local dev server\n-----------------------------\n\nDefaultbashnpm start\n\nRuns at **<http://localhost:3000>** with hot module replacement. Note: do not run `npm start` inside a Claude Code session — start it in a separate terminal and leave it running.\n\n5. Supported AI coding tools\n----------------------------\n\nVSCode + Claude Code is the primary and default setup — the rules, skills, and hooks are authored for it. Cursor and GitHub Copilot are also supported via a cross-AI sync script that mirrors the same governance rules to each tool's expected config location.\n\n**VSCode + Claude Code (recommended):** install [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code) globally (`npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code`), then `claude login` (opens a browser; use your work email — contact your team lead if you don't have an Anthropic account).\n\n**Cursor:** open the repo root — picks up rules automatically from `.cursor/rules/` (auto-generated, do not edit directly).\n\n**GitHub Copilot (VS Code):** project rules are at `.github/copilot-instructions.md` and `.vscode/instructions/` (also auto-generated).\n\n6. Verify TypeScript\n--------------------\n\nDefaultbashnpx tsc --noEmit\n\nRun again after any change touching `overrides/`. TypeScript only — no `.js`/`.jsx` files belong there.\n\nNext steps\n----------\n\nSee the [Claude Code CLI — Developer Onboarding Guide](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/304775192) for session rules, prompting guide, and recovery steps, and the [Branching Guide (GitFlow)](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/304840733) page for the branching model.",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "page_width": "fixed-width"
  }
}"}

{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "261881857",
    "title": "Component Override Strategy",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/261881857",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 4,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "How Zimmermann customisation works on top of the base Salesforce PWA Kit template. For general naming/testing/styling conventions, see the parent [Development Standards](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/261750785) page.\n\nThe overrides pattern\n---------------------\n\nThis project is a **slim overrides project** on top of `@salesforce/retail-react-app` v10.\n\nThe base template lives in `node_modules/@salesforce/retail-react-app/`. It cannot and must not be edited — changes there would be wiped out by the next `npm install`. All Zimmermann customisations live in `overrides/app/`, which the PWA Kit module resolution layer checks before falling back to the base template. For example:\n\nDefaultnonenode\\_modules/@salesforce/retail-react-app/app/components/product-tile/index.jsx\n↑ base template — never touch this\noverrides/app/components/product-tile/index.tsx\n↑ Zimmermann override — this is what runs in production\n\nIf a file exists in `overrides/app/`, it replaces the base template version entirely. If it does not exist in `overrides/app/`, the base template version runs.\n\nOverriding vs Creating a new file\n---------------------------------\n\n### A. Overriding a base component\n\nUse this when changing a component that already exists in the base template (e.g. `product-tile`, `header`, `checkout`).\n\n1. **Copy the file** — copy the base file to the mirrored path under `overrides/`, convert it to TypeScript, and add a temporary `console.log` to confirm the override is rendering: Defaultbashcp node\\_modules/@salesforce/retail-react-app/app/components/product-tile/index.jsx \\\n   overrides/app/components/product-tile/index.tsx\n2. Convert the file to TypeScript (`.js` → `.ts`/`.tsx`), and update import references within the new file.\n3. Run `npx tsc --noEmit` to confirm no TypeScript errors.\n\nTo test the override is working: add `console.log('[overrides] product-tile/index.tsx')` near the top, start the dev server, open the relevant page, and confirm the log fires in the browser console. Remove the `console.log` and make functional changes only after the override is confirmed rendering.\n\n**Keep the filename identical** (kebab-case) — overrides of Salesforce files keep the upstream filename exactly.\n\n### B. Creating a new Zimmermann-specific component\n\nUse this when building something that doesn't exist in the base template (e.g. a \"Back In Stock\" button, a loyalty points badge).\n\nNew custom components go under `overrides/app/components/custom/`, e.g.:\n\nDefaultnoneoverrides/app/components/custom/backInStockButton/\nindex.tsx ← the component\nindex.stories.tsx ← Storybook story (required)\nindex.test.ts ← Vitest (required if the component has non-trivial logic)\n\nNaming: `camelCase` for Zimmermann-authored folders and files (e.g. `backInStockButton/`, `useWishlist.ts`, `formatPrice.ts`).\n\nBranch first\n------------\n\nEvery change goes on a feature branch — never directly on `develop` or `main`.\n\nDefaultbashgit checkout develop\ngit pull origin develop\ngit checkout -b feature/SFCC-123\\_short-description\n\nBranch naming: `feature/{JIRA-KEY}_{short-description}`. Full branching model: see the [Branching Guide (GitFlow)](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/304840733) page under Development Standards & Tooling.",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "emoji": "1f680",
    "page_width": "fixed-width"
  }
}"}

{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "304840733",
    "title": "Branching Guide (GitFlow)",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/304840733",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 3,
    "attachments": [
      {
        "id": "att305135617",
        "type": "attachment",
        "status": "current",
        "title": "BranchingDiagram.png",
        "media_type": "application/octet-stream",
        "file_size": 99442,
        "download_url": "/rest/api/content/304840733/child/attachment/att305135617/download"
      }
    ],
    "content": {
      "value": "This branching model is based on **GitFlow**. (See the attached diagram on this page.)\n\nThe six branch types\n--------------------\n\n| Branch | Created from | Merges into | Claude Code? | CI that runs |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `feature/*` | `develop` | `develop` (via PR) | ✅ Yes | `qa.yml` (unit tests) on PR |\n| `fix/*` | `develop` | `develop` (via PR) | ✅ Yes | `qa.yml` (unit tests) on PR |\n| `hotfix/*` | `main` | `main`**and**`develop` | ✅ Yes | `qa.yml` (unit tests) on PR |\n| `release/**` | `develop` | `main` and `develop` | ⚠️ Read-only skills only | `digital-sit.yml` (full gate) + `qa.yml` (cross-browser) |\n| `fix/release-{v}-*` | `release/**` | `release/**` (→ `develop` via release back-merge) | ✅ Yes | `digital-sit.yml` (full gate) on PR |\n| `develop` | — | `release/**` + SIT | ❌ No | `digital-sit.yml` (full gate) on every push |\n\nClaude Code is physically blocked from writing to `develop` or `main` via a branch-lock hook in the repo's `.claude/settings.json`.\n\nFeature branches — day-to-day branch\n------------------------------------\n\n**When:** every task, bug fix, or experiment.\n\nbashwide4000git checkout develop\ngit pull origin develop\ngit checkout -b feature/SFCC-123\\_short-description\n\n**Naming format:**`feature/{JIRA-KEY}_{short-description}`. Good: `feature/SFCC-456_add-back-in-stock-button`. Bad: `feature/my-changes`, `feature/fix`.\n\n**When done:** open a PR targeting `develop`. `qa.yml` unit tests run automatically. One approval required before merge — `develop` is a shared integration point and your PR must not break it for anyone else.\n\n**Before every session:**`git branch --show-current` (confirm branch) and `git pull origin develop` (merges happen frequently, even if you pulled this morning).\n\nHotfix branches — production emergencies only\n---------------------------------------------\n\n**When:** a defect in production needs an immediate fix outside the sprint cycle.\n\n**Create from** `main` **— not** `develop`**:**\n\nbashwide4000git checkout main\ngit pull origin main\ngit checkout -b hotfix/SFCC-999\\_critical-fix-description\n\n**Why from** `main` **not** `develop`**:**`develop` has unreleased sprint work in progress. A hotfix branched from `develop` would accidentally include unreleased features. Branching from `main` keeps the fix clean and scoped to production state only.\n\n**Tier 1 rules still apply** — even under deadline pressure, do not modify Tier 1 files without owner approval.\n\n**When done:** PR into `main`. After merge, the fix **must** also be back-merged to `develop` to prevent the regression re-appearing next sprint. (Merge commit vs. cherry-pick mechanism — confirm with the Integration Lead.)\n\nRelease branches — deployment targets, not working branches\n-----------------------------------------------------------\n\n**When:** a sprint is complete and a release is being promoted.\n\nRelease branches are created by squad leads or CI from a tagged `develop` state — developers do not create these. They exist as deployment targets for the SIT gate and cross-browser tests.\n\n**Pattern:**`release/v0.1.0`, `release/digital/v1.0.0`.\n\n**What happens here:** the full Digital SIT gate runs on every PR targeting `release/**`; BrowserStack cross-browser tests run on every PR targeting `release/**`; no new feature work goes here — only emergency release fixes after squad lead approval.\n\n### Making a code fix on a release branch\n\nDo not edit the release branch directly for code changes. Branch off it instead so the CI gate runs against the fix before it lands.\n\nbashwide4000# Step 1 — branch from the release branch, not develop\ngit checkout release/v0.1.0\ngit pull origin release/v0.1.0\ngit checkout -b fix/release-v0.1.0\\_short-description\n# Step 2 — make the fix\n# Step 3 — PR back into the release branch (not develop)\ngh pr create --base release/v0.1.0 --title \"fix(checkout): SFCC-999 payment error on SIT\"\n\nOnce merged, squad leads apply the same fix to `develop` via a second PR to prevent the regression re-appearing next sprint.\n\n**When direct commits to a release branch are acceptable:** only for changes with no logic — copy corrections, version number bumps, a boolean flag toggle. These require explicit squad lead approval before committing.\n\n**Claude Code on** `release/**`**:** supervised mode — plan first, blast-radius check, confirm before every edit. Code logic changes must go through a `fix/release-*` sub-branch as described above.\n\ndevelop — the integration branch\n--------------------------------\n\n**No direct commits.** All changes arrive via PR from a `feature/*` branch. `develop` receives a deployment to Digital Dev and SIT on every push — a bad push breaks the integration environment for both squads simultaneously, which is why the PR review gate exists.\n\nmain — production state\n-----------------------\n\n**No direct commits.** All changes arrive via a release promotion or a `hotfix/*` PR. `main` always reflects the current production code. Tagging main with a semver tag (`v0.1.0`) is done by the release tooling at `scripts/tagRelease.sh`.\n\nTagging and versioning\n----------------------\n\n| Stage | Version format | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Pre-go-live sprints | `v0.{sprint}.0` | `v0.1.0`, `v0.2.0` |\n| Go-live | `v1.0.0` | `v1.0.0` |\n| Post-go-live patches | `v1.0.{patch}` | `v1.0.1`, `v1.0.2` |\n| Post-go-live features | `v1.{minor}.0` | `v1.1.0` |\n\nTags are created by running `scripts/tagRelease.sh v{version}` from `main` after the release PR is merged — this creates both `v{version}` and `release/digital/v{version}` tags.\n\nSprint boundary tags (`sprint/{N}/{date}`) are created automatically by CI when `scripts/sprint.txt` is updated. Update the file to the new sprint number when a sprint begins — increment it in a commit on `develop`.\n\nCommon git commands\n-------------------\n\nbashwide4000# Start a new feature\ngit checkout develop && git pull origin develop\ngit checkout -b feature/SFCC-123\\_my-task\n# Keep your branch up to date during a long sprint\ngit fetch origin\ngit rebase origin/develop # or: git merge origin/develop\n# Check what files Claude Code changed in this session\ngit diff --name-only\n# Revert a specific file back to develop state\ngit checkout origin/develop -- path/to/file.ts\n# Revert everything (use carefully — discards all uncommitted changes)\ngit checkout .\n# Open a PR from the CLI\ngh pr create --base develop --title \"feat(checkout): SFCC-123 add back in stock button\"",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "emoji": "20ac58f1-f8df-438f-aade-bfe1e8e67f9c",
    "page_width": "max"
  }
}"}

{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "305561601",
    "title": "PWA CI/CD Pipeline (GitHub Actions)",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/305561601",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 3,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "> **Scope of this page:** this covers CI/CD for the **PWA/frontend repo** (`zimmermann-pwa`) — GitHub Actions workflows, branch protection, and commit conventions. For the **SFCC backend** cartridge build/deploy pipeline, see SFCC Build and Deployment Process – Headless Architecture.\n\nTrigger matrix\n--------------\n\n| Event | Workflow | Jobs that run |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Push to `develop` | `digital-sit.yml` | install → playwright-p0, lighthouse-ci, contract-tests, bundle-size → sit-gate → deploy-sit → tag-sit-deploy, tag-sprint |\n| PR targeting `release/**` | `digital-sit.yml` | install → playwright-p0, lighthouse-ci, contract-tests, bundle-size → sit-gate |\n| PR targeting `develop` or `release/**` | `qa.yml` | unit-tests, storybook-test (every PR, parallel) → e2e-browserstack (release/\\*\\* only) |\n| PR opened against any branch | `protected-file-guard.yml` | tier-1-guard, tier-2-guard |\n| Weekly cron (Mondays, 1am UTC) | `weekly-agents.yml` | quality-sweep, contentful-drift-audit, changelog-reminder |\n| Weekly cron (Mondays, 0:00 UTC) | `upstream-release-watch.yml` | check-for-new-release |\n\nJob dependency graph — `digital-sit.yml`\n----------------------------------------\n\nnonewide4000install\n├── playwright-p0\n├── lighthouse-ci\n├── contract-tests\n└── bundle-size\n└── (all four) → sit-gate\n├── deploy-playwright-report (develop pushes only)\n└── deploy-sit (develop pushes, sit-gate success only)\n├── tag-sit-deploy\n└── tag-sprint (only when scripts/sprint.txt changes)\n\nWhat each job does\n------------------\n\n| Job | File | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `install` | `digital-sit.yml` | Installs and caches node\\_modules |\n| `playwright-p0` | `digital-sit.yml` | Runs P0 Playwright suite (Chromium, SIT env) |\n| `lighthouse-ci` | `digital-sit.yml` | Runs LHCI against SIT env, uploads to trend server |\n| `contract-tests` | `digital-sit.yml` | MuleSoft API contract assertion (placeholder until spec confirmed) |\n| `bundle-size` | `digital-sit.yml` | Checks JS/CSS bundle thresholds via bundlewatch |\n| `sit-gate` | `digital-sit.yml` | Composite gate — fails if any of the four signal jobs failed |\n| `unit-tests` | `qa.yml` | Vitest unit tests at repo root (`npm test`) |\n| `storybook-test` | `qa.yml` | Storybook `play` interaction tests and `@storybook/addon-a11y` checks headless |\n| `deploy-playwright-report` | `digital-sit.yml` | Publishes Playwright HTML report to GitHub Pages |\n| `deploy-sit` | `digital-sit.yml` | Pushes and activates build on SIT MRT environment |\n| `tag-sit-deploy` | `digital-sit.yml` | Creates `sit/deploy/{date}-{sha}` git tag for NewStore traceability |\n| `tag-sprint` | `digital-sit.yml` | Creates `sprint/{N}/{date}` tag on sprint rollover |\n| `e2e-browserstack` | `qa.yml` | Cross-browser E2E on BrowserStack (Chrome, Safari, Firefox, Edge) |\n| `tier-1-guard` | `protected-file-guard.yml` | Blocks Tier 1 file changes without explicit owner approval |\n| `tier-2-guard` | `protected-file-guard.yml` | Requires a second reviewer for Tier 2 changes |\n| `quality-sweep` | `weekly-agents.yml` | Opens an issue for components/hooks missing tests or stories |\n| `contentful-drift-audit` | `weekly-agents.yml` | Opens an issue for orphaned `*ContentfulDefinition.ts` files |\n| `changelog-reminder` | `weekly-agents.yml` | Comments on open release-branch PRs without a changelog entry |\n| `check-for-new-release` | `upstream-release-watch.yml` | Opens an issue when `@salesforce/retail-react-app` ships a new release |\n\nFor the environment tiers each of these jobs deploys to, see Environment Topology & Promotion Path.\n\nBranch protection rules\n-----------------------\n\n### `develop`\n\n1. Require a pull request before merging\n2. Required approvals: **2** (one from owning squad, one cross-squad check)\n3. Require status checks to pass before merging — add check: `unit-tests` (from `qa.yml`)\n4. Require branches to be up to date before merging\n5. Do not allow bypassing the above settings\n\n### `main`\n\n1. Require a pull request before merging\n2. Required approvals: **2** (both squad leads must approve release merges)\n3. Require branches to be up to date before merging\n4. Do not allow bypassing the above settings\n\nHotfix PRs into `main` still require 2 approvals — under deadline pressure, get the second approver on a call rather than bypassing the gate.\n\n### `release/**`\n\nRequired check: `Digital SIT Gate` — composite job depending on all four SIT signal jobs.\n\n| Signal | Tool | Threshold | Blocks? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Playwright P0 journeys | Playwright | 100% pass on P0 suite, all storefront configs | Yes |\n| Lighthouse CI | LHCI | Perf ≥ 80, A11y ≥ 90, Best Practices ≥ 90, drop > 5 pts = blocked | Yes |\n| MuleSoft contract tests | Pact / OpenAPI | Any schema breach = blocked | Yes (pending spec source confirmation) |\n| Bundle size | bundlewatch | main.js < 180 kB, vendor.js < 250 kB, main.css < 50 kB | Yes |\n\nVitest and Storybook interaction + a11y tests run in `qa.yml` (every PR to `develop`) — they gate the PR merge, not the environment deploy.\n\nConfiguring in GitHub: Settings → Branches → Add branch protection rule → branch pattern `release/**` → require status checks → add `Digital SIT Gate` → require branches up to date → do not allow bypassing (no admin overrides).\n\n### STAGE convergence gate\n\nSTAGE promotion requires both the Digital SIT gate green and NewStore SIT to be complete. This is a **manual gate** enforced by the delivery team before raising a STAGE promotion PR, not an automated GitHub status check. Checklist before any STAGE PR:\n\n* [ ] `Digital SIT Gate` check is green on the release branch\n* [ ] NewStore team has confirmed SIT testing is complete for this sprint\n* [ ] Integration Lead and both squad leads have signed off\n\n### GitHub Pages — Playwright report publishing\n\nSettings → Pages → set source to **GitHub Actions**. The Playwright HTML report publishes automatically to GitHub Pages on every push to `develop` once this is enabled.\n\nCommit message quality enforcement\n----------------------------------\n\nEnforced at two layers:\n\n**Layer 1 — local** `commit-msg` **hook** (Husky-installed via `npm install`). Rejects commits not matching `type(optional-scope): short description`. Valid types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`, `upstream-sync`. Merge commits, reverts, fixups, squash commits, and WIP prefixes pass through automatically.\n\n**Layer 2 — CodeRabbit PR review** (`coderabbit.yaml`). Checks every commit in every PR for conventional commits compliance, catching commits pushed without the local hook installed.\n\nThis matters because the changelog skill (`/generate-changelog`) parses commit history by conventional type to produce the four-audience release changelog — non-conventional commits are classified \"Uncategorised\" and can't be automatically routed.\n\nSecrets required by CI\n----------------------\n\n| Workflow | Secret | Purpose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `digital-sit.yml` | `LHCI_SERVER_BASE_URL` | Lighthouse CI trend server |\n| `digital-sit.yml` | `LHCI_TOKEN` | Lighthouse CI auth |\n| `digital-sit.yml` | `LHCI_TARGET_URL_SIT` | SIT storefront URL for Lighthouse |\n| `digital-sit.yml` | `MRT_USER` | MRT push / activate auth |\n| `digital-sit.yml` | `MRT_API_KEY` | MRT push / activate auth |\n| `qa.yml` | `BROWSERSTACK_USERNAME` | BrowserStack account |\n| `qa.yml` | `BROWSERSTACK_ACCESS_KEY` | BrowserStack auth |\n| `qa.yml` | `RETAIL_APP_HOME_SIT` | SIT storefront URL for E2E tests |\n| `qa.yml` | `PWA_E2E_USER_EMAIL` | Registered test account email |\n| `qa.yml` | `PWA_E2E_USER_PASSWORD` | Registered test account password |\n| `qa.yml` | `E2E_STANDARD_PRODUCT_SKU` | In-stock test product |\n| `qa.yml` | `E2E_VARIANT_PRODUCT_SKU` | Multi-variant test product |\n| `qa.yml` | `E2E_OOS_PRODUCT_SKU` | Out-of-stock test product |\n| `qa.yml` | `E2E_SALE_PRODUCT_SKU` | Sale product |\n| `qa.yml` | `E2E_FIXTURE_ORDER_NUMBER` | Completed order for history tests |\n\nOne-time GitHub setup (Teams for CODEOWNERS, installing CodeRabbit, verifying the guard): see the 7.1c CI/CD & Environment Setup Checklist page.\n\nFor the failure-triage playbook (what to do when a specific gate check fails), see SIT Quality Gates — Thresholds & Troubleshooting.",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "emoji": "20ac58f1-f8df-438f-aade-bfe1e8e67f9c",
    "page_width": "max"
  }
}"}

