    "version": 2,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "This guide is for developers using **Claude Code CLI** (terminal-based) to connect to Jira and Confluence. For **Claude Desktop** users, see Claude Desktop — Atlassian MCP Setup Guide instead — the two tools use different configuration mechanisms.\n\nHow it's wired up\n-----------------\n\nThe `zimmermann-pwa` repo ships a committed `.mcp.json` that wires up `mcp-atlassian` (Jira + Confluence tools) for Claude Code. It references environment variables only — no credentials are checked in — so each developer supplies their own Atlassian account.\n\nClaude Code does **not** read `.env` automatically. This repo uses [direnv](https://direnv.net/) (via a committed, secret-free `.envrc`) to auto-load `.env` whenever you `cd` into the project, so you don't have to source it manually every terminal session.\n\nSetup checklist\n---------------\n\n* [ ] Install `uv` (<https://docs.astral.sh/uv/>) — `.mcp.json` invokes the server via `uvx`, which needs it on your `PATH`\n* [ ] Install `direnv` (`brew install direnv` on macOS) and hook it into your shell — add `eval \"$(direnv hook zsh)\"` (or the equivalent for your shell) to your shell profile, then restart your terminal\n* [ ] Generate a personal API token at <https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens>\n* [ ] Add these six vars to your local `.env` (never `.env.sample`): `JIRA_URL`, `JIRA_USERNAME`, `JIRA_API_TOKEN`, `CONFLUENCE_URL`, `CONFLUENCE_USERNAME`, `CONFLUENCE_API_TOKEN`\n* [ ] Run `direnv allow` once in the repo root — direnv requires explicit approval of `.envrc` per repo, as a safety check\n* [ ] First time only: Claude Code will prompt you to approve the project-scoped `mcp-atlassian` server — approve it, then exit and start a new session (adding/approving an MCP server does not hot-reload into a running session)\n* [ ] Run `/mcp` in a Claude Code session to confirm `mcp-atlassian` shows as connected\n\nWithout `direnv`, you'd need to manually run `set -a && source .env && set +a` before every new terminal session before starting `claude` — `direnv` removes that step.\n\nIf you work across multiple Atlassian accounts/clients, this is per-project by design: `.mcp.json` carries no account info, so a different repo's `.env` can point at a different Jira/Confluence instance without touching this file.",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "emoji": "c92fb136-f084-45a3-9b2a-503c8101ba82",
    "page_width": "max"
  }
}"}

{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "275251202",
    "title": "Claude Desktop — Atlassian MCP Setup Guide",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/275251202",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 6,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "This guide walks through setting up the **mcp-atlassian** server on Claude Desktop so that Claude can read and write directly to Confluence and Jira — no copy-paste, no tab switching.\n\n**What you get after setup:** Claude can create and update Confluence pages, create Jira stories, transition issue statuses, search the backlog, and add comments — all from a natural language prompt in Claude Desktop.\n\n**MCP package used:**[sooperset/mcp-atlassian](https://github.com/sooperset/mcp-atlassian) (open source, MIT licence)\n\n---\n\nBefore You Start — What You Need\n--------------------------------\n\n* Claude Desktop installed ([download here](https://claude.ai/download))\n* An Atlassian Cloud account with access to Jira and Confluence\n* An Atlassian API token (generated in step 2 below)\n* `uv` and the `mcp-atlassian` tool installed on your machine (covered in step 1 below)\n\n---\n\nStep 1 — Install Prerequisites\n------------------------------\n\n### macOS\n\nmacOS requires **Homebrew** to install `uv` (the Python package runner that powers the MCP server).\n\n**Install Homebrew** (skip if already installed):\n\nOpen Terminal and run:\n\nDefaultbash/bin/bash -c \"$(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Homebrew/install/HEAD/install.sh)\"\n\nFollow the on-screen prompts. When it finishes, run this to confirm it worked:\n\nDefaultbashbrew --version\n\n**Install `uv` via Homebrew:**\n\nDefaultbashbrew install uv\n\nConfirm it installed:\n\nDefaultbashuvx --version\n\nYou should see a version number printed. If you do, you're ready to install mcp-atlassian itself.\n\n**Install mcp-atlassian as a persistent, pinned tool:**\n\nDon't run mcp-atlassian via `uvx mcp-atlassian` — that rebuilds a brand new Python environment from scratch every single time Claude Desktop starts, which can cause intermittent connection failures and 100+ packages re-downloading on every launch. Instead, install it once as a permanent tool:\n\nDefaultbashuv tool install \"mcp-atlassian==0.21.1\"\n\nConfirm the install location — you'll need this exact path in Step 4:\n\n| OS | Command to check | Typical path |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| macOS | `ls ~/.local/bin/mcp-atlassian` | `/Users/<your-username>/.local/bin/mcp-atlassian` |\n| Windows | `uv tool dir --bin` | `C:\\Users\\<your-username>\\.local\\bin\\mcp-atlassian.exe` |\n\nKeep this path handy for Step 4.\n\n---\n\n### Windows\n\nWindows requires **winget** (built into Windows 10/11) or the standalone installer.\n\n**Option A — Install via winget (recommended):**\n\nOpen **PowerShell** (not Command Prompt) and run:\n\nDefaultpowershellwinget install --id=astral-sh.uv -e\n\n**Option B — Install via PowerShell script:**\n\nDefaultpowershellpowershell -ExecutionPolicy ByPass -c \"irm https://astral.sh/uv/install.ps1 | iex\"\n\nAfter installation, close and reopen PowerShell, then confirm:\n\nDefaultpowershelluvx --version\n> **Note for Windows users:** If `uvx` is not found after install, you may need to add it to your PATH. The installer will tell you the path to add — go to System Properties → Environment Variables → Path → New, paste the path, and click OK.\n\n**Install mcp-atlassian as a persistent, pinned tool:**\n\nDon't run mcp-atlassian via `uvx mcp-atlassian` — that rebuilds a brand new Python environment from scratch every single time Claude Desktop starts, which can cause intermittent connection failures and 100+ packages re-downloading on every launch. Instead, install it once as a permanent tool:\n\nDefaultpowershelluv tool install \"mcp-atlassian==0.21.1\"\n\nConfirm the install location — you'll need this exact path in Step 4:\n\n| OS | Command to check | Typical path |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| macOS | `ls ~/.local/bin/mcp-atlassian` | `/Users/<your-username>/.local/bin/mcp-atlassian` |\n| Windows | `uv tool dir --bin` | `C:\\Users\\<your-username>\\.local\\bin\\mcp-atlassian.exe` |\n\nKeep this path handy for Step 4.\n\n---\n\nStep 2 — Generate Your Atlassian API Token\n------------------------------------------\n\nYour API token authenticates Claude against Jira and Confluence. One token works for both.\n\n1. Go to: <https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens>\n2. Click **Create API token**\n3. Give it a label — e.g. `Claude Desktop MCP`\n4. Click **Create** then **Copy** the token immediately — you won't see it again\n\n> **Keep this token private.** It has the same access as your Atlassian account. Do not share it, commit it to Git, or paste it into Confluence or Slack.\n\n---\n\nStep 3 — Find Your Atlassian URLs\n---------------------------------\n\nYou need two URLs — your Jira base URL and your Confluence base URL.\n\n| What | Format | Example |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Jira URL | `https://your-org.atlassian.net` | `https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net` |\n| Confluence URL | `https://your-org.atlassian.net/wiki` | `https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki` |\n\nThe org name is the subdomain you see when logged into Atlassian. In our case it is `zimmermannworkboard`.\n\n---\n\nStep 4 — Edit the Claude Desktop Config File\n--------------------------------------------\n\nClaude Desktop uses a configuration file to know which MCP servers to load. You need to open that file and add the Atlassian MCP block to it.\n\nThis step involves editing a JSON file. JSON is a structured text format — it uses curly braces `{}`, square brackets `[]`, colons `:`, and commas `,` in specific places. The most important rule: **the structure must be exact**. A single missing comma or misplaced bracket will silently break the setup.\n\nFollow the steps below carefully and you will be fine.\n\n---\n\n### 4a — Open the config file using Claude Desktop's built-in shortcut\n\nYou do not need to navigate to the file manually. Claude Desktop has a shortcut that opens it for you.\n\n1. Open Claude Desktop\n2. Go to **Claude menu → Settings** (macOS) or the hamburger menu → **Settings** (Windows)\n3. Click the **Developer** tab\n4. Click **Edit Config**\n\nThis opens the `claude_desktop_config.json` file in your default text editor (usually Notepad on Windows, TextEdit on macOS) and creates it automatically if it does not exist yet.\n\n> **If TextEdit opens on macOS:** Go to **Format → Make Plain Text** before editing. TextEdit in rich text mode will corrupt the file.\n\n---\n\n### 4b — Understand what you are looking at\n\nWhen the file opens, you will see one of three things:\n\n**Scenario A — The file is empty or brand new:**\n\nThe file will be blank or contain just `{}`. This means no MCP servers are configured yet.\n\n**Scenario B — The file has content including an `mcpServers` section:**\n\nOther MCP servers are already configured. You will see something like:\n\nDefaultjson{\n\"mcpServers\": {\n\"some-other-server\": {\n\"command\": \"npx\",\n\"args\": [\"-y\", \"some-package\"]\n}\n}\n}\n\n**Scenario C — The file has content but no `mcpServers` section:**\n\nClaude Desktop may have other settings saved but no MCP servers configured yet. You will see something like:\n\nDefaultjson{\n\"someOtherSetting\": true,\n\"anotherSetting\": \"value\"\n}\n\nNotice there is no `\"mcpServers\"` key anywhere in the file. This needs different handling — see 4c below.\n\n---\n\n### 4c — Add the Atlassian MCP configuration\n\nAll three scenarios below use the same `mcp-atlassian` block. The `\"command\"` value is the exact path you confirmed at the end of Step 1 — **not** the bare word `uvx`. Running it this way means Claude Desktop launches your pinned, already-installed copy directly, instead of rebuilding a fresh Python environment from scratch on every single startup.\n\n**Scenario A — File is empty or just `{}`:**\n\nSelect all and replace the entire contents with this, filling in your own values:\n\nDefaultjson{\n\"mcpServers\": {\n\"mcp-atlassian\": {\n\"command\": \"<path-from-step-1>\",\n\"args\": [],\n\"env\": {\n\"JIRA\\_URL\": \"https://your-org.atlassian.net\",\n\"JIRA\\_USERNAME\": \"your.email@company.com\",\n\"JIRA\\_API\\_TOKEN\": \"paste-your-api-token-here\",\n\"CONFLUENCE\\_URL\": \"https://your-org.atlassian.net/wiki\",\n\"CONFLUENCE\\_USERNAME\": \"your.email@company.com\",\n\"CONFLUENCE\\_API\\_TOKEN\": \"paste-your-api-token-here\"\n}\n}\n}\n}\n\n---\n\n**Scenario B — File already has an `mcpServers` section:**\n\nAdd the Atlassian block inside the existing `\"mcpServers\"` section, after the last existing server. Add a comma after the previous server's closing `}` to separate them:\n\nDefaultjson{\n\"mcpServers\": {\n\"some-other-server\": {\n\"command\": \"npx\",\n\"args\": [\"-y\", \"some-package\"]\n},\n\"mcp-atlassian\": {\n\"command\": \"<path-from-step-1>\",\n\"args\": [],\n\"env\": {\n\"JIRA\\_URL\": \"https://your-org.atlassian.net\",\n\"JIRA\\_USERNAME\": \"your.email@company.com\",\n\"JIRA\\_API\\_TOKEN\": \"paste-your-api-token-here\",\n\"CONFLUENCE\\_URL\": \"https://your-org.atlassian.net/wiki\",\n\"CONFLUENCE\\_USERNAME\": \"your.email@company.com\",\n\"CONFLUENCE\\_API\\_TOKEN\": \"paste-your-api-token-here\"\n}\n}\n}\n}\n\nNote the comma after `some-other-server`'s closing `}` — this is required. The **last** server in the list never gets a trailing comma.\n\n---\n\n**Scenario C — File has content but no `mcpServers` section:**\n\nYou need to add the entire `\"mcpServers\"` block inside the existing outer `{}` braces. Add a comma after the last existing setting to separate it, then add the new block before the final closing `}`:\n\nBefore: Defaultjson{\n\"someOtherSetting\": true,\n\"anotherSetting\": \"value\"\n}\n\nAfter: Defaultjson{\n\"someOtherSetting\": true,\n\"anotherSetting\": \"value\",\n\"mcpServers\": {\n\"mcp-atlassian\": {\n\"command\": \"<path-from-step-1>\",\n\"args\": [],\n\"env\": {\n\"JIRA\\_URL\": \"https://your-org.atlassian.net\",\n\"JIRA\\_USERNAME\": \"your.email@company.com\",\n\"JIRA\\_API\\_TOKEN\": \"paste-your-api-token-here\",\n\"CONFLUENCE\\_URL\": \"https://your-org.atlassian.net/wiki\",\n\"CONFLUENCE\\_USERNAME\": \"your.email@company.com\",\n\"CONFLUENCE\\_API\\_TOKEN\": \"paste-your-api-token-here\"\n}\n}\n}\n}\n\nThe key rules here: the comma goes after `\"anotherSetting\": \"value\"` to separate it from the new block, and the entire `\"mcpServers\": { ... }` block sits inside the outermost `{}` — it is not added after the final `}`.\n\n---\n\n### 4d — Fill in your values\n\nReplace the placeholder text with your actual details:\n\n| Placeholder | Replace with |\n| --- | --- |\n| `<path-from-step-1>` | macOS: the path from `ls ~/.local/bin/mcp-atlassian` (e.g. `/Users/jsmith/.local/bin/mcp-atlassian`). Windows: the path from `uv tool dir --bin` plus `\\mcp-atlassian.exe`, with backslashes doubled (e.g. `C:\\\\Users\\\\jsmith\\\\.local\\\\bin\\\\mcp-atlassian.exe`) |\n| `https://your-org.atlassian.net` | Your Atlassian URL from Step 3 |\n| `your.email@company.com` | The email address on your Atlassian account |\n| `paste-your-api-token-here` | The API token you copied in Step 2 |\n\nThe same API token goes in both `JIRA_API_TOKEN` and `CONFLUENCE_API_TOKEN`.\n\n> **Windows users:** JSON requires backslashes to be escaped as `\\\\`. `C:\\Users\\jsmith\\.local\\bin\\mcp-atlassian.exe` must be typed as `C:\\\\Users\\\\jsmith\\\\.local\\\\bin\\\\mcp-atlassian.exe` in the config file.\n\n---\n\n### 4e — Check your work before saving\n\nCommon mistakes that break the setup:\n\n| Mistake | Wrong | Correct |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Missing comma between entries | `} {` | `},` then new entry |\n| Trailing comma on the last entry | `\"token\",` then `}` | No comma on the last item |\n| Adding `mcpServers` outside the outer braces | `} \"mcpServers\": {` | `mcpServers` must be inside the outermost `{ }` |\n| Smart/curly quotes from a rich text editor | `\"uvx\"` (curly) | `\"uvx\"` (straight) |\n| Single backslashes in a Windows path | `\"C:\\Users\\...\"` | `\"C:\\\\Users\\\\...\"` |\n\nIf you are unsure, copy the entire file contents, go to [jsonlint.com](https://jsonlint.com), paste it in, and click **Validate JSON**. It will tell you exactly which line has an error.\n\n---\n\n### 4f — Save the file\n\n* **macOS:**`Cmd + S`\n* **Windows:**`Ctrl + S`\n\n---\n\nStep 5 — Restart Claude Desktop\n-------------------------------\n\nMCP servers are only loaded when Claude Desktop starts up. You must fully quit and reopen it — opening a new conversation is not enough.\n\n**macOS:** Press `Cmd + Q` to quit, then reopen Claude Desktop from your Applications folder.\n\n**Windows:** Right-click the Claude icon in the system tray at the bottom-right of your screen → **Quit**. Then reopen Claude Desktop from the Start menu.\n\n---\n\nStep 6 — Verify the Connection\n------------------------------\n\nOpen a new Claude Desktop conversation and type:\n\nDefaultnoneList the Jira projects I have access to.\n\nor\n\nDefaultnoneSearch Confluence for pages about \"environment strategy\".\n\nIf Claude returns results from your Atlassian instance, the MCP is connected and working.\n\nYou can also confirm by looking at the bottom of the chat window — you should see a small hammer 🔨 icon with a number next to it. That number is the count of MCP tools currently available to Claude. Click it to see the full list.\n\n---\n\nTroubleshooting\n---------------\n\n### Claude says it has no tools / can't access Atlassian\n\n* Confirm you fully quit and restarted Claude Desktop — not just opened a new chat\n* Open the config file again (Settings → Developer → Edit Config) and check it looks correct\n* Paste the file contents into [jsonlint.com](https://jsonlint.com) to check for JSON errors\n* Confirm `uvx --version` works in your terminal — if not, `uv` is not installed or not on your PATH\n\n### Authentication errors\n\n* Check your API token is pasted correctly with no extra spaces at the start or end\n* Verify your email matches your Atlassian account login exactly\n* Confirm the Jira URL does **not** end in `/wiki` and the Confluence URL **does**\n* Make sure your Atlassian account has access to the spaces and projects you are trying to reach\n\n### Scenario D — Already set up with the old bare `uvx` config?\n\nIf you configured mcp-atlassian before July 2026 using `\"command\": \"uvx\", \"args\": [\"mcp-atlassian\"]`, you may see intermittent disconnects or mcp-atlassian re-downloading 100+ packages on every Claude Desktop launch. Redo the **\"Install mcp-atlassian\"** part of Step 1 and update your config as shown in Step 4c — this replaces the bare `uvx` command with a pinned, persistent install and fixes both issues.\n\n### View Claude Desktop MCP logs\n\nIf something is not working, these log files show exactly what is happening:\n\n**macOS (Terminal):**\n\nDefaultbashtail -f ~/Library/Logs/Claude/mcp\\*.log\n\n**Windows (PowerShell):**\n\nDefaultpowershellGet-Content \"$env:APPDATA\\Claude\\logs\\mcp\\*.log\" -Wait\n\n---\n\nSecurity Reminders\n------------------\n\n* **Never share your API token.** It has full access to your Atlassian account.\n* **Never commit the config file to Git.** It contains your token in plain text.\n* **Rotate your token** if you think it has been exposed — generate a new one at [id.atlassian.com](https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens) and update the config file.\n* Claude Desktop MCP acts with **your permissions** — it can only access what your Atlassian account can access.\n\n---\n\nReference\n---------\n\n| Resource | Link |\n| --- | --- |\n| mcp-atlassian GitHub | <https://github.com/sooperset/mcp-atlassian> |\n| mcp-atlassian full docs | <https://mcp-atlassian.soomiles.com> |\n| Atlassian API token management | <https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens> |\n| Claude Desktop download | <https://claude.ai/download> |\n| JSON validator | <https://jsonlint.com> |\n\n---\n\n*Last updated: July 2026 | Zimmermann Digital Modernisation — Accenture Song*",
