      "value": "> **Who this is for:** Developers and Testers\n> **Stages covered:** Stage 4 (Playwright Spec), Stage 5 (BrowserStack Execution), Stage 6 (Defect Triage & Fix Branch)\n> **Prerequisite:** Zephyr test cases must be in **Ready for Execution** status before the spec is written. See [Guide 2](../277708807) if that step has not been completed.\n\n---\n\nWhat This Guide Covers\n----------------------\n\nThis guide covers everything from writing the Playwright spec through to a failed test resulting in a merged fix. It explains the execution matrix, how BrowserStack Automate connects to the CI pipeline, how results sync back to Zephyr Scale, and how the defect triage agent classifies failures and raises fix branches.\n\n---\n\nStage 4 — Writing the Playwright Spec\n-------------------------------------\n\n### Where Specs Live\n\nAll Zimmermann test specs live in:\n\n```\ne2e/tests/zimm/[feature-area]/[STORY-KEY]-[description].[priority].spec.js\n```\n\nExamples:\n\n```\ne2e/tests/zimm/checkout/ZIM-042-guest-checkout.p0.spec.js\ne2e/tests/zimm/pdp/ZIM-031-variant-selection.p1.spec.js\n```\n\nThe `.p0` / `.p1` suffix determines which CI gate picks up the spec:\n- **`.p0`** — runs in the Digital SIT gate on every merge to `develop`\n- **`.p1`** — runs in the full BrowserStack matrix on PRs to `release/**`\n\n### Selectors — Page Object Model\n\nSelectors must never be written inline in the spec file. They live in the Page Object file for that feature area:\n\n```\ne2e/support/pages/CheckoutPage.js\ne2e/support/pages/ProductPage.js\ne2e/support/pages/AccountPage.js\n```\n\nSelector priority (in order — use the highest available):\n1. ARIA role + accessible name: `page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Place Order' })`\n2. Label: `page.getByLabel('Email address')`\n3. Visible text: `page.getByText('Order confirmed')`\n4. `data-testid` attribute: `page.getByTestId('order-number')`\n5. Never: CSS class, DOM position, tag name alone\n\nThis doubles as a continuous accessibility check — if you cannot select an element by role or label, that element likely has an accessibility problem worth flagging.\n\n### Multi-Region Fixtures\n\nEvery spec is parameterised by `SITE_ID` — not hardcoded to one storefront. The site fixture supplies the correct `baseUrl`, currency, checkout owner, and consent defaults.\n\n```\nimport { SITE_CONFIGS, activeSiteKey } from '../../fixtures/site'\n\nconst site = SITE_CONFIGS[activeSiteKey()]\n\ntest.describe(`Guest checkout — ${site.name}`, () => {\n  test('AC-01: Guest completes checkout with valid card', async ({ page }) => {\n    await page.goto(`${site.baseUrl}/cart`)\n    // ...\n  })\n})\n```\n\nRun a spec against a specific storefront locally:\n\n```\nSITE_ID=uk npx playwright test ZIM-042-guest-checkout.p0.spec.js\n```\n\n### Generating the Spec from Zephyr Test Cases\n\nUse Claude Desktop with the MCP connected to pull the test cases directly from Zephyr — no copy-pasting required.\n\n**Step 1 — Ask Claude to retrieve the Zephyr test cases for the story:**\n\n```\nUsing the Atlassian MCP, retrieve all Zephyr test cases linked to Jira story ZIM-042 \nand show me the full detail of each one — steps, expected results, priority, and labels.\n```\n\nClaude will fetch and display all test cases linked to that story. Confirm the list looks right and that all cases are in Ready for Execution status before continuing.\n\n**Step 2 — Ask Claude to generate the Playwright spec:**\n\n```\nUsing those Zephyr test cases, generate a Playwright spec scaffold that follows \nthe Zimmermann e2e conventions:\n- File path: e2e/tests/zimm/checkout/ZIM-042-guest-checkout.p0.spec.js\n- Page Object Model — all selectors go in CheckoutPage.js, not inline in the spec\n- Parameterised by SITE_ID using the site fixture\n- Test names must match the Zephyr test case names exactly\n- One test per Zephyr test case\n```\n\nClaude generates the scaffold from the test cases it already retrieved. The spec will have placeholder selectors (e.g. `// TODO: add to CheckoutPage.js`) — the Developer's job is to replace these with correct selectors in the Page Object file, then confirm the spec passes locally against the AU storefront before committing.\n\n**If you need to adjust the output**, give Claude specific instructions:\n\n| What needs changing | What to say |\n| --- | --- |\n| A test step needs a more specific selector hint | \"In the test for TC-03, the payment submission step — note that the button selector should use role 'button' with name 'Place Order'\" |\n| A test should be P0 not P1 | \"Change the file suffix to .p0 — this is a smoke scenario\" |\n| The Page Object file doesn't exist yet | \"Also generate a skeleton CheckoutPage.js with placeholder methods for each action in the spec\" |\n| A test case covers multiple regions | \"TC-05 is labelled ALL — wrap its describe block to iterate across all seven SITE\\_IDs from the fixture\" |\n\nClaude keeps the Zephyr test case detail in context, so corrections are fast and targeted.\n\n---\n\nStage 5 — BrowserStack Execution\n--------------------------------\n\n### When BrowserStack Runs\n\nBrowserStack Automate is triggered by GitHub Actions on **PRs to `release/**`**. It does not run on every `develop` merge — the SIT gate handles that using headless Playwright directly.\n\nThe CI job is defined in `.github/workflows/qa.yml`.\n\n### The Execution Matrix\n\nEvery spec tagged `.p1` or above runs across:\n\n| Storefronts (7) | Browsers (4) |\n| --- | --- |\n| AU | Chrome (latest) |\n| UK | Safari (latest) |\n| US Domestic | Firefox (latest) |\n| US International | Edge (latest) |\n| EU Domestic |  |\n| EU International |  |\n| ME |  |\n\nThis is 7 × 4 = **28 parallel execution contexts** per spec. What takes a human tester 3 days runs in under 2 hours.\n\n### `.browserstack.yml` Configuration\n\nThe BrowserStack config is scoped to Zimmermann tests only — it does not run the upstream Salesforce test suite:\n\n```\nbrowsers:\n  - browser: chrome\n    browser_version: latest\n  - browser: safari\n    browser_version: latest\n  - browser: firefox\n    browser_version: latest\n  - browser: edge\n    browser_version: latest\n\nplatforms:\n  - os: Windows\n    os_version: 11\n  - os: OS X\n    os_version: Ventura\n\nproject: Zimmermann-SFCC\nbuild: Sprint-${SPRINT_NUMBER}\nname: ${GITHUB_REF_NAME}-${GITHUB_SHA}\n\ntestSuite: e2e/tests/zimm\n```\n\nThe `Sprint-${SPRINT_NUMBER}` build tag makes results traceable back to Zephyr Scale — every BrowserStack session is tagged to the sprint, which maps to the Jira sprint.\n\n### Result Sync to Zephyr Scale\n\nAfter each BrowserStack run completes, the CI pipeline posts results back to Zephyr Scale via the Zephyr Scale API. Each test result — pass, fail, or skip — is recorded against the matching Zephyr test case using the test case name as the link key.\n\n* Pass: test case execution record shows green in Zephyr\n* Fail: test case execution record shows red, with a link to the BrowserStack session recording\n* Skip: test case execution record shows skipped (e.g. if the storefront is not yet live)\n\nThe Tester does not manually update Zephyr execution records — the sync is automatic.\n\n---\n\nStage 6 — Defect Triage & Fix Branch\n------------------------------------\n\n### How Failures Are Classified\n\nWhen a BrowserStack run produces failures, the triage agent runs against the failure report and classifies each failure into one of three categories:\n\n| Classification | Meaning | Agent Action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **Code defect** | The application is not behaving as specified — a bug in the Zimmermann codebase | Agent creates a fix branch, investigates, implements a fix (Tier 2–4 files only), raises PR |\n| **Environment issue** | Test failed due to a missing environment dependency — wrong test data, service unavailable, sandbox not seeded | Agent raises a Jira defect with classification \"Environment\" and assigns to the relevant team |\n| **Test data issue** | Test failed because a fixture value is stale or incorrect — e.g. a product SKU that no longer exists in the test environment | Agent raises a Jira defect with classification \"Test Data\" and assigns to the Tester who owns the fixture |\n\n### The Fix Branch Model\n\nFor code defects, the agent's behaviour is tier-gated:\n\n| File Tier | Agent Action |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Tier 1** — design tokens, shared hooks, runtime config, CI workflows | Agent **stops**. Raises a Jira defect tagged Tier 1 and assigns to the Lead Developer. No automated fix attempt. |\n| **Tier 2** — global shell components, shared product components, hooks with 5+ consumers | Agent creates a fix branch and raises a PR, but adds a Tier 2 warning label and requests a second reviewer |\n| **Tier 3–4** — squad-owned and feature-scoped files | Agent creates a fix branch, investigates, implements a fix, and raises a PR with full investigation detail |\n\n**In all cases: no PR is merged without human code review.** The agent can never merge its own PR. This is enforced by GitHub branch protection rules on `develop` and all `release/**` branches.\n\n### What the Agent Raises in Jira\n\nFor every failure (regardless of classification), the agent creates a Jira defect with:\n\n* **Summary:** `[STORY-KEY] [Test Case Name] — [Failure classification] — [Browser/Storefront]`\n* **Description:** The full failure message, stack trace, and a link to the BrowserStack session recording\n* **Labels:** `defect`, the classification (`code-defect` / `env-issue` / `test-data`), the affected storefront, and the severity\n* **Severity:** P1 (blocks story sign-off) or P2 (does not block but must be resolved before release)\n* **Link to:** the original Jira story and the Zephyr test case\n\n### What the Agent Raises in GitHub\n\nFor code defects on Tier 2–4 files, the PR raised by the agent includes:\n\n* **Branch name:** `fix/agent-[STORY-KEY]-TC-[N]-[short-description]`\n* **PR title:** `[STORY-KEY] Fix: [Test case name] — [Browser/Storefront]`\n* **PR body:** root cause analysis, files changed and why, link to the Zephyr test case, link to the BrowserStack session, file tier confirmation, and (for Tier 2) a note that a second reviewer is required\n\n### Reviewing and Merging Agent PRs\n\nThe Developer assigned to review an agent PR must:\n\n1. Read the root cause analysis — confirm it makes sense\n2. Review the code change — does it correctly fix the failure without introducing new problems?\n3. Check that only the expected files were changed — the agent should not have touched Tier 1 files or files outside the failure's scope\n4. Run the failing test locally against the fix branch to confirm it passes\n5. Merge if satisfied — or close the PR and fix manually if the agent's approach is wrong\n\nIf the agent's fix is wrong, close the PR, add a comment explaining what the agent got wrong, and fix manually. The incorrect fix attempt is useful context — do not delete it.\n\n---\n\nSeverity Reference\n------------------\n\n| Severity | Meaning | Action |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| P0 | Blocks smoke — checkout or core journey completely broken | Immediate fix required before any deployment |\n| P1 | Story cannot be signed off — acceptance criterion fails | Fix before story moves to Done |\n| P2 | Edge case or non-blocking defect | Fix before release candidate is cut |\n| P3 | Cosmetic or low-impact | Backlog for prioritisation |\n\n---\n\nQuick Reference — Stages 4–6\n----------------------------\n\n```\nZephyr test cases: Ready for Execution\n        │\n        ▼\n\"Retrieve all Zephyr test cases linked to ZIM-042\"\n        │\n        ▼\n\"Generate a Playwright spec scaffold from those test cases\"\n        │\n        ▼\nDeveloper adds correct selectors to Page Object file\n        │\n        ▼\nSpec passes locally → PR raised to release/**\n        │\n        ▼\nGitHub Actions triggers BrowserStack\n        │\n        ▼\n28 parallel contexts run (7 storefronts × 4 browsers)\n        │\n        ▼\nResults sync to Zephyr Scale automatically\n        │\n   ┌────┴────┐\n   │         │\n PASS      FAIL\n   │         │\n   ▼         ▼\nStory    Triage agent classifies failure\nmoves    │\nforward  ├─ Environment / Test Data: Jira defect raised, assigned\n         │\n         └─ Code defect:\n              ├─ Tier 1: Jira defect, assigned to Lead Dev\n              └─ Tier 2–4: Fix branch + PR raised by agent\n                    │\n                    ▼\n              Developer reviews PR ◀ HUMAN GATE 3\n                    │\n              Merge or close and fix manually\n```\n\n---\n\n*Last updated: June 2026 | Zimmermann Digital Modernisation — Accenture Song*",
      "format": "markdown"
    },
    "emoji": "1f680",
    "page_width": "fixed-width"
  }
}"}

