      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 1,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "Epics in Jira – Overview\n------------------------\n\n### What is an Epic?\n\nAn **Epic** represents a larger piece of work that delivers a meaningful outcome or capability for the business. In this SFCC project, an Epic typically:\n\n* Spans **multiple user stories / tasks / bugs**\n* Delivers a **coherent feature, enhancement, or business outcome**\n* May span **multiple sprints**, but should be small enough to complete within a release\n\n---\n\n### When to create an Epic (vs a Story)\n\nCreate an **Epic** when:\n\n* The work cannot be delivered in a **single story or task**\n* Multiple stories/tasks/bugs are needed across **different components or teams**\n* The business wants to **track progress and delivery** at a feature or outcome level\n* The work is expected to span **more than one sprint** or is a key release item\n\nUse a **Story** instead when:\n\n* The work is clearly **single-sprint**, with a focused, testable outcome\n* It does **not** need to be tracked independently on the roadmap\n* It is a small change that **doesn’t require multiple linked items**\n\n---\n\n### Required / expected fields for an Epic\n\nFor consistency in this SFCC Jira project, Epics should have:\n\n* **Summary** – short, clear description of the outcome\n\n  + e.g. `Checkout – Reduce cart abandonment`\n* **Description** – include:\n\n  + Business context & problem statement\n  + High-level scope / out of scope\n  + Success criteria / KPIs if known\n* **Labels** – for grouping (e.g. `sfcc`, `frontend`, `backend`, `performance`, `release-2025Q1`)\n* **Dependencies / Risks** – note any known external teams, technical dependencies, or risks\n\n---\n\n### Linking work to Epics\n\nTo keep planning and reporting accurate:\n\n* All **Stories**, **Tasks**, and **Bugs** that contribute to an Epic **must be linked** to it\n\n  + Use the standard **Epic Link / Parent** relationship configured in this Jira project\n* Each Story should **only belong to one Epic** (avoid splitting ownership)\n* When new work arises that is clearly part of an existing Epic:\n\n  + **Search for the Epic first**, then link the new Story/Task/Bug to it\n\n### Using the workflow day-to-day\n\n* **Update status** when the *overall state of the Epic* changes (not for every story move)\n* Keep **description, scope, and links** current so reporting and roadmap views stay accurate",
      "format": "markdown"
    }
  }
}"}

